    {
      "id": "p0162-b004",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "In Japanese, Onionsan ends sentences in a way that's associated with childishness, silliness, and awkward nerdiness. The Japanese script also treats the \"san\" part of\"Onionsan\" as the Japanese name suffix -san, which is roughly equivalent to \"Mr.\" or \"Ms.\" In English. However, it's improper to add -san when saying your own name, so this localization choice adds another layer of social awkwardness to the character.",
        "raw": "In Japanese, Onionsan ends sentences in a way that's associated with\nchildishness, silliness, and awkward nerdiness. The Japanese script also treats\nthe \"san\" part of\"Onionsan\" as the Japanese name suffix -san, which is roughly\nequivalent to \"Mr.\" or \"Ms.\" In English. However, it's improper to add -san\nwhen saying your own name, so this localization choice adds another layer of\nsocial awkwardness to the character.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.30127748,
          0.34708571,
          0.91062373,
          0.44981429
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.29232069,
          0.34637143,
          0.91265705,
          0.45088571
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          19,
          20,
          21,
          22,
          23,
          24
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "日语中，Onionsan 的句尾带着孩子气、傻乎乎又不擅交际的宅气。译文还把“Onionsan”里的 san 视作日语姓名后缀 -san，大致相当于英语的 Mr. 或 Ms.（先生／女士）。然而，说自己的名字时不应加 -san，所以这一处理又给角色添了一层不通人情世故的感觉。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": [
