    {
      "id": "p0162-b007",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "In Japanese entertainment, characters sometimes refer to themselves by using their own name rather than a pronoun. This happens in other languages too, of course, but it's extraordinarily common in Japanese media. The use of one's own name instead of a pronoun can make a character seem childish, endearing, or uncivilized. Examples of such pronoun-less characters include:",
        "raw": "In Japanese entertainment, characters sometimes refer to themselves\nby using their own name rather than a pronoun. This happens in other\nlanguages too, of course, but it's extraordinarily common in Japanese\nmedia. The use of one's own name instead of a pronoun can make a\ncharacter seem childish, endearing, or uncivilized. Examples of such\npronoun-less characters include:",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.33051666,
